  "starterCode": "def sql_group_by(records: list, group_key: str, agg_key: str) -> list:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **GROUP BY** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\ndef sql_group_by(records: list, group_key: str, agg_key: str) -> list:\n    g = {}\n    for r in records: g.setdefault(r[group_key], []).append(r[agg_key])\n    return [{group_key: k, 'count': len(v), 'sum': round(float(sum(v)), 2), 'avg': round(float(sum(v) / len(v)), 2)} for k, v in sorted(g.items())]\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
